SS3_A = {
"Biology": [
 ("Genetics: Mendelian Inheritance",
  [("Introduction", ["Genetics is the study of heredity — how characteristics pass from parents to offspring. Gregor Mendel, the father of genetics, worked with garden peas and discovered the laws of inheritance."]),
   ("Key terms", ["<b>Gene</b> — a section of DNA controlling one characteristic; <b>allele</b> — an alternative form of a gene (T for tall, t for short).", "<b>Genotype</b> — the genetic make-up (TT, Tt, tt); <b>phenotype</b> — the observable appearance (tall, short).", "<b>Dominant</b> — an allele that expresses itself even when paired with a recessive one; <b>recessive</b> — expressed only when two copies are present (homozygous).", "<b>Homozygous</b> — two identical alleles (TT or tt); <b>heterozygous</b> — two different alleles (Tt)."]),
   ("Mendel's laws", ["<b>Law of segregation</b> — the two alleles of each gene separate during gamete formation, so each gamete carries one allele.", "<b>Law of independent assortment</b> — alleles of different genes are inherited independently and combine at random (for genes on different chromosomes)."]),
   ("Monohybrid cross", ["Tall (TT) x dwarf (tt) → all F1 tall (Tt). Self the F1 → F2 ratio 3 tall : 1 dwarf (genotype 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt). A test cross (with a homozygous recessive) reveals whether a dominant phenotype is TT or Tt."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define gene, allele, genotype and phenotype.", "State the law of segregation.", "In a monohybrid cross Tt x Tt, give the genotypic and phenotypic ratios.", "What is a test cross and when is it used?"])]),
 ("Genetic Crosses & Heredity Problems",
  [("Introduction", ["Solving heredity problems requires a Punnett square — a grid that shows all possible combinations of parental gametes. This week practises monohybrid and dihybrid crosses, including blood groups."]),
   ("Steps in solving", ["1. Write the parental genotypes. 2. Work out the gametes of each parent. 3. Draw the Punnett square. 4. Fill the boxes. 5. Read off the genotypic and phenotypic ratios."]),
   ("Worked example", ["In guinea pigs, black fur (B) dominates white (b). A heterozygous black male (Bb) mates with a white female (bb). Gametes: B, b x b, b. Offspring: Bb, Bb, bb, bb → 1 black : 1 white (1:1). If both parents are Bb → 3 black : 1 white."]),
   ("Blood groups (ABO)", ["Alleles IA and IB are codominant; i is recessive. Genotypes: IAIA or IAi (group A); IBIB or IBi (group B); IAIB (group AB); ii (group O). A group O parent (ii) and group AB parent (IAIB) can produce A and B children only."]),
   ("Sex determination", ["Human females are XX, males XY. The male produces X and Y gametes in equal numbers; a cross shows a 1:1 ratio of girls to boys — the father's gamete decides the sex of the child."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["A tall pea plant (Tt) is crossed with a dwarf plant (tt). Show the Punnett square and state the phenotypic ratio.", "Can two group A parents have a group O child? Explain with a cross.", "Who determines the sex of a child and how?", "Distinguish between monohybrid and dihybrid inheritance."])]),
 ("DNA, RNA & Protein Synthesis",
  [("Introduction", ["Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) is the genetic material of living cells. It carries the instructions for making proteins — the molecules that do most of the work in the cell."]),
   ("Structure of DNA", ["DNA is a double helix of two strands wound round each other. Each strand is a chain of nucleotides; a nucleotide has a phosphate group, a sugar (deoxyribose) and a nitrogenous base. Bases pair specifically (complementary base pairing): <b>A–T</b> and <b>C–G</b> (in DNA; in RNA A pairs with U). A gene is a sequence of bases coding for a protein."]),
   ("DNA replication", ["The double helix unwinds; each strand acts as a template; new complementary bases are added by enzymes so two identical DNA molecules are formed. This happens before cell division and explains how genetic information is copied exactly."]),
   ("RNA and protein synthesis", ["RNA is single-stranded with the sugar ribose and uracil instead of thymine. <b>Transcription</b>: in the nucleus, a messenger RNA (mRNA) copy of one gene is made. <b>Translation</b>: the mRNA moves to a ribosome in the cytoplasm, where transfer RNA (tRNA) brings amino acids in the order coded — groups of three bases (codons) specify each amino acid. The chain folds into a protein."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Describe the structure of DNA.", "What is complementary base pairing?", "Distinguish between transcription and translation.", "How does a gene determine a protein?"])]),
 ("Genetic Engineering & Biotechnology",
  [("Introduction", ["Genetic engineering is the artificial manipulation of an organism's genes to produce desired traits — inserting, deleting or modifying DNA. Biotechnology uses living organisms or their products for human benefit."]),
   ("Tools and techniques", ["<b>Restriction enzymes</b> cut DNA at specific points; <b>ligase</b> joins DNA fragments. <b>Plasmids</b> (small circular bacterial DNA) are used as vectors (carriers). <b>PCR</b> (polymerase chain reaction) makes millions of copies of a DNA fragment; <b>electrophoresis</b> separates DNA fragments by size, used in DNA fingerprinting."]),
   ("Applications", ["<b>Medicine</b> — human insulin for diabetics (made by bacteria), vaccines, gene therapy, screening for inherited diseases.", "<b>Agriculture</b> — genetically modified (GM) crops resistant to pests, drought or herbicides (Bt maize), and improved yields.", "<b>Industry</b> — enzymes in detergents, fermentation for bread, beer and antibiotics (penicillin from Penicillium).", "<b>Forensics</b> — DNA fingerprinting identifies criminals and proves paternity."]),
   ("Ethical concerns", ["Safety of GM foods, effects on biodiversity, gene patents, 'designer babies', privacy of genetic information, and the risk of biological weapons. Society needs laws and ethics to guide the technology."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define genetic engineering.", "Name the enzymes used in gene splicing.", "How is human insulin produced today?", "State two applications of DNA fingerprinting.", "Mention two ethical concerns of genetic engineering."])]),
 ("Variation & Evolution",
  [("Introduction", ["Variation is the differences that exist between individuals of the same species. Evolution is the gradual change in living things over generations, from simple to more complex forms."]),
   ("Types of variation", ["<b>Continuous</b> — a smooth range of small differences (height, weight, skin colour, shoe size), controlled by many genes and influenced by the environment.", "<b>Discontinuous</b> — distinct categories with no intermediates (blood groups, tongue rolling, presence of ear lobes), controlled by one or few genes."]),
   ("Causes of variation", ["Genetic: mutation, crossing over during meiosis, random fertilisation, independent assortment. Environmental: nutrition, climate, exercise, light. Most variation results from both heredity and environment."]),
   ("Evolution by natural selection (Darwin)", ["Individuals in a population show variation; more offspring are produced than can survive; there is competition for resources (struggle for existence); individuals with favourable variations survive, reproduce and pass the trait on (survival of the fittest); over many generations the species changes. Evidence for evolution: fossils, comparative anatomy, vestigial organs, embryology and molecular similarities. Lamarck proposed the inheritance of acquired characteristics — now rejected."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Differentiate between continuous and discontinuous variation with examples.", "Name two causes of variation.", "Outline Darwin's theory of natural selection.", "Give three evidences of evolution."])]),
 ("Adaptation",
  [("Introduction", ["Adaptation is the possession of structures or behaviours that enable an organism to survive and reproduce in its environment. Adaptation arises from natural selection acting on variation."]),
   ("Types of adaptation", ["<b>Structural</b> — body features: the camel's hump (fat store), long legs of the heron (wading), webbed feet of ducks (swimming), flat teeth of herbivores (grinding), spines of cacti (water loss), gills of fish (breathing in water).", "<b>Behavioural</b> — actions: migration of birds, hibernation, aestivation, nocturnal activity, herding, protective display, parental care.", "<b>Physiological</b> — internal processes: production of antifreeze by arctic fish, salt glands of sea birds, camouflage (cryptic coloration) and mimicry as protective adaptations."]),
   ("Adaptation to specific environments", ["<b>Xerophytes</b> (desert): thick cuticle, sunken stomata, reduced leaves, water storage, long roots. <b>Hydrophytes</b> (water): large air spaces, floating leaves, reduced roots. <b>Halophytes</b> (salt): salt toleration. <b>Desert animals</b>: nocturnal, efficient kidneys, humps in camels, burrowing."]),
   ("Adaptation and evolution", ["Adaptations are the visible results of natural selection — the environment 'selects' the traits that work. They help explain biodiversity and why species occupy particular niches."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define adaptation.", "Give two structural adaptations of a fish and a cactus.", "Distinguish between structural and behavioural adaptation.", "Why do deserts have few but highly adapted species?"])]),
],
"Chemistry": [
 ("Solubility & Salts",
  [("Introduction", ["Solubility is the maximum amount of a solute that dissolves in a given amount of solvent at a particular temperature, to form a saturated solution. Solubility is often expressed as grams of solute per 100 g (or 100 cm³) of water."]),
   ("Saturated, unsaturated and supersaturated solutions", ["<b>Saturated</b> — no more solute will dissolve at that temperature (dynamic equilibrium between dissolving and crystallising).", "<b>Unsaturated</b> — more solute can still dissolve.", "<b>Supersaturated</b> — contains more dissolved solute than the saturation level; it is unstable and crystallises on seeding."]),
   ("Solubility curves", ["A solubility curve plots solubility against temperature. Most solids become more soluble as temperature rises (the curve rises); gases become less soluble as temperature rises (the curve falls). The curves allow calculation of the mass of crystals that form on cooling."]),
   ("Preparation of salts", ["Salts are prepared by: (1) acid + metal → salt + hydrogen (using a metal above hydrogen in the reactivity series); (2) acid + base/metal oxide → salt + water; (3) acid + alkali → salt + water (titration, for soluble salts); (4) acid + carbonate → salt + water + carbon dioxide; (5) direct combination of elements (e.g. iron + sulphur → iron(II) sulphide). Insoluble salts are made by precipitation (double decomposition). Solubility rules decide the method: all nitrates are soluble; all chlorides are soluble except AgCl, PbCl2, Hg2Cl2; all sulphates are soluble except BaSO4, PbSO4, CaSO4; carbonates, sulphites and hydroxides are insoluble except those of sodium, potassium and ammonium."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define solubility.", "What is a saturated solution?", "How does temperature affect the solubility of a gas?", "Which method is used to prepare soluble copper(II) sulphate from insoluble copper(II) oxide?", "State the solubility rule for chlorides."])]),
 ("Volumetric Analysis",
  [("Introduction", ["Volumetric analysis (titrimetry) is the measurement of the volumes of solutions that react exactly with one another. It is used to find unknown concentrations, e.g. the molarity of an acid or alkali."]),
   ("Apparatus and procedure", ["<b>Burette</b> delivers the titrant drop by drop; <b>pipette</b> (with a pipette filler) measures a fixed volume of the solution in the conical flask; <b>conical flask</b> holds the solution being titrated; <b>indicator</b> shows the end point (methyl orange for strong acid–weak alkali; phenolphthalein for strong alkali–weak acid). Read the burette meniscus at eye level; repeat the titration until two concordant titres are obtained (differing by 0.1 cm³ or less); always rinse the burette and pipette with the solutions they will hold."]),
   ("Calculations", ["Key relations: moles = concentration (mol/dm³) x volume (dm³). At the end point, moles of acid ÷ moles of alkali = ratio from the balanced equation; balance the equation first. Example: NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H2O (1:1). 25.0 cm³ of NaOH is neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ HCl → moles HCl = 0.100 x 0.020 = 0.002; moles NaOH = 0.002; concentration NaOH = 0.002 ÷ 0.025 = 0.080 mol/dm³."]),
   ("Standard solutions", ["A standard solution has a known concentration, made by dissolving a precisely weighed sample in a known volume of water. The primary standard must be pure, stable and of high molar mass (e.g. anhydrous sodium carbonate for acids); NaOH is not a primary standard because it absorbs moisture and carbon dioxide."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define volumetric analysis.", "State the use of each of: burette, pipette, indicator.", "What are concordant titres?", "Why is NaOH not a primary standard?", "25.0 cm³ of 0.080 mol/dm³ HCl neutralises 20.0 cm³ of NaOH. Find the molarity of the NaOH."])]),
 ("Atomic Theory (Advanced)",
  [("Introduction", ["The modern atomic theory builds on Dalton's ideas with the discovery of sub-atomic particles and the nucleus. This week deepens the treatment: orbitals, isotopes, electronic structure and the mass spectrometer."]),
   ("Atomic structure refined", ["Atoms consist of protons and neutrons in a very small, dense nucleus, with electrons occupying energy levels (shells) and sub-levels (s, p, d orbitals). The maximum electrons per shell follow 2n² (2, 8, 18, 32). The valence electrons determine chemical properties and the position of the element in the periodic table."]),
   ("Isotopes", ["Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same proton number but different mass numbers (different numbers of neutrons). They have identical chemical properties but differ in physical properties. Examples: H-1, H-2, H-3; C-12 and C-14; Cl-35 and Cl-37. Isotopes are used in medicine (radioactive I-131 for the thyroid), archaeology (C-14 dating) and industry. Relative atomic mass is the weighted average of the masses of the isotopes — it explains non-integer atomic masses (e.g. Cl = 35.5)."]),
   ("The mass spectrometer", ["It separates isotopes by mass/charge ratio: a vapourised sample is ionised by a beam of electrons; the ions are accelerated and deflected by a magnetic field; the lighter ions deflect more, and the detector records a peak for each — the positions give masses, the peak heights give abundances."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Sketch the electronic structure of chlorine (Z = 17) and state its group.", "Why do isotopes of an element have the same chemical properties?", "Explain why the atomic mass of chlorine is 35.5.", "Briefly describe how a mass spectrometer separates isotopes."])]),
 ("Chemical Kinetics",
  [("Introduction", ["Chemical kinetics is the study of the rates of chemical reactions — how fast reactants turn into products, and which factors control the speed."]),
   ("Measuring rate", ["Rate = change in concentration (or amount) ÷ time. It is measured by tracking the disappearance of a reactant or the appearance of a product over time: volume of gas evolved (by gas syringe), loss in mass (if a gas escapes), colour change, or titration of samples at intervals. The rate decreases with time as reactants are used up — the concentration–time graph is a curve flattening to zero."]),
   ("Factors affecting rate", ["<b>Concentration</b> — higher concentration → more frequent collisions → faster (collision theory).", "<b>Temperature</b> — a 10 °C rise roughly doubles the rate; particles gain kinetic energy and more collisions have energy above the activation energy.", "<b>Surface area</b> — smaller particles (more area) react faster (powdered limestone vs lumps).", "<b>Catalyst</b> — lowers activation energy by providing an alternative pathway; the catalyst is unchanged at the end.", "Pressure (for gases) and light (for photochemical reactions) also matter."]),
   ("Collision theory", ["Reactions occur only when particles collide with energy equal to or greater than the activation energy (Ea), and in the correct orientation. Anything that increases the frequency or energy of effective collisions speeds up the reaction."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define rate of reaction.", "State four factors that affect the rate of a reaction with reasons.", "What is activation energy?", "Explain why a catalyst speeds up a reaction without being used up.", "Why do reactions slow down as they proceed?"])]),
 ("Equilibrium",
  [("Introduction", ["Many reactions are reversible: they can go forward and backward. Chemical equilibrium is the state in which the forward and backward rates are equal, so the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant (though both reactions continue)."]),
   ("Dynamic equilibrium", ["Equilibrium is dynamic — not static: molecules keep converting in both directions at equal rates. It can be reached only in a closed system. At equilibrium the position (how far towards products) is described by the equilibrium constant, Keq; K > 1 favours products, K < 1 favours reactants."]),
   ("Le Chatelier's principle", ["If a system at equilibrium is disturbed (by a change in concentration, pressure or temperature), the equilibrium shifts in the direction that reduces the disturbance.", "<b>Concentration</b> — adding a reactant shifts right; removing a product shifts right. <b>Pressure</b> — increasing pressure shifts towards the side with fewer gas molecules. <b>Temperature</b> — raising it favours the endothermic direction; lowering it favours the exothermic direction. <b>Catalyst</b> — speeds up the approach to equilibrium but does NOT shift the position."]),
   ("Application: the Haber process", ["N2 + 3H2 ⇌ 2NH3 (exothermic forward). Industrial conditions: 450 °C, 200 atm, iron catalyst. The compromise temperature gives a reasonable rate, the high pressure pushes the equilibrium right, and the catalyst speeds the reaction without changing the yield."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define dynamic equilibrium.", "State Le Chatelier's principle.", "For N2 + 3H2 ⇌ 2NH3 (exothermic), what happens when pressure is increased and why?", "Does a catalyst increase the yield at equilibrium? Explain.", "Why is 450 °C used in the Haber process if the reaction is exothermic?"])]),
 ("Electrolysis & Electrochemical Cells",
  [("Introduction", ["Electrolysis is the decomposition of a compound (electrolyte) by the passage of an electric current, using inert electrodes. Electrochemical cells, by contrast, convert chemical energy into electrical energy."]),
   ("Electrolysis principles", ["Electrolytes are compounds that conduct electricity in solution or when molten because they contain mobile ions — acids, alkalis and salts. Non-electrolytes (sugar, ethanol) do not conduct. At the cathode, cations gain electrons (reduction); at the anode, anions lose electrons (oxidation); the electrode where oxidation occurs is the anode."]),
   ("Predicting products", ["In molten lead(II) bromide: Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb (cathode); 2Br⁻ → Br2 + 2e⁻ (anode). In dilute aqueous sodium chloride with inert electrodes: H2 at the cathode and O2 at the anode because the discharge series selects the ion easier to discharge (in solution, H⁺ before Na⁺; OH⁻ before Cl⁻ in dilute solution, but Cl⁻ in concentrated brine). Copper refining uses copper anodes and a copper cathode with copper(II) sulphate electrolyte."]),
   ("Faraday's laws & uses", ["The mass of a substance deposited is proportional to the quantity of electricity passed (Q = It), and to the chemical equivalent. Uses: extraction of reactive metals (aluminium, sodium), electroplating (gold/silver plating), refining of copper, production of chlorine and caustic soda, and anodising."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define electrolysis.", "Which ions discharge at each electrode in molten PbBr2?", "Predict the products of electrolysis of concentrated brine.", "State four industrial applications of electrolysis."])]),
],
"Economics": [
 ("Agricultural Economics",
  [("Introduction", ["Agricultural economics studies how scarce resources are allocated in the production, distribution and consumption of farm products. In Nigeria agriculture remains a major source of employment and foreign exchange."]),
   ("Characteristics of agriculture in Nigeria", ["Subsistence or semi-commercial production; small holdings with traditional tools; dependence on weather (rain-fed); low capital investment; land tenure problems; poor storage and processing (post-harvest losses); inadequate credit and extension services; drift of young labour to the cities."]),
   ("Problems of agricultural development", ["Land fragmentation (inheritance) and land tenure insecurity; inadequate finance and high cost of inputs; poor rural infrastructure (roads, electricity, storage); pests and diseases; soil degradation and erosion; the farmer–herder conflicts that disrupt production; smuggling and fluctuating commodity prices."]),
   ("Marketing and contribution", ["Agricultural marketing covers assembling, grading, processing, transporting and distributing farm produce; the marketing boards of the past stabilised prices. The sector contributes to GDP, provides food and raw materials, employs labour, earns export revenue (cocoa, rubber, cashew, sesame) and supports rural development."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["State five characteristics of Nigerian agriculture.", "Explain how land tenure hinders agricultural development.", "What is agricultural marketing?", "Mention three ways agriculture contributes to the Nigerian economy."])]),
 ("Petroleum & the Nigerian Economy",
  [("Introduction", ["Petroleum (crude oil) was discovered at Oloibiri in 1956 and has dominated the Nigerian economy since the 1970s. It is the leading source of government revenue and foreign exchange."]),
   ("Benefits of petroleum", ["Huge government revenue (petroleum profits tax, royalties) that funds the budget; foreign exchange earnings; employment in exploration, refining and distribution; provision of petroleum products for industry and transport; revenues for infrastructure; membership of OPEC gives Nigeria influence; the development of a petrochemical industry (fertiliser, plastics)."]),
   ("Problems", ["Over-dependence on oil makes the economy vulnerable to price shocks; oil revenue breeds corruption and the neglect of agriculture; crude oil is exported while Nigeria imports refined products because refineries operate below capacity; gas flaring pollutes the environment; oil spills damage farmland and fishing in the Niger Delta; price volatility; the Dutch Disease — oil revenue raises the exchange rate and hurts other exports."]),
   ("Solutions & OPEC", ["Diversification into agriculture, manufacturing and services; local refining and value addition; transparency in oil revenue management (NEITI, PIA 2021); gas monetisation; job creation outside oil. OPEC is the cartel of oil-exporting countries — Nigeria's membership gives a voice in world oil policy."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Why is Nigeria called a mono-economy?", "List four benefits of petroleum to Nigeria.", "State three problems caused by over-dependence on oil.", "How can Nigeria reduce its dependence on oil?"])]),
 ("Manufacturing & Industry",
  [("Introduction", ["Industry is the organised production of goods and services. Manufacturing is the secondary sector — turning raw materials into finished goods. Industrialisation is the process of building a manufacturing base in an economy."]),
   ("Types of industry", ["<b>Primary</b> — extraction of raw materials (farming, mining, fishing).", "<b>Secondary (manufacturing)</b> — processing and fabrication (textiles, cement, food processing, steel).", "<b>Tertiary</b> — services (banking, transport, trade, tourism). Industries can also be classified by scale (small, medium, large), by output (heavy vs light) and by ownership (private, public, mixed)."]),
   ("Location factors", ["Nearness to raw materials; availability of power and water; labour supply and skills; transport and market; capital; government policy; climate and land. Nigeria's industries cluster in Lagos, Ogun, Kano, Kaduna and Aba (Nnewi for auto parts)."]),
   ("Importance and problems of manufacturing", ["Importance: employment, higher value added, foreign exchange savings, technology transfer, reduced dependence on imports, stimulates agriculture and services. Problems: inadequate power supply; poor infrastructure; dependence on imported raw materials and machines; shortage of skilled labour and capital; smuggling and dumping of cheap imports; policy instability; high cost of production."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define industry and state its types.", "Mention four factors that determine industrial location.", "State three importance of manufacturing.", "Why do Nigerian industries perform below capacity?"])]),
 ("Transport & Communication",
  [("Introduction", ["Transport is the movement of people and goods from place to place; communication is the transmission of information and messages. Both are infrastructure services that support every other sector of the economy."]),
   ("Modes of transport", ["<b>Road</b> — most used in Nigeria (about 80% of freight): flexible, door-to-door, but congested and costly to maintain.", "<b>Rail</b> — cheap for bulk, heavy and long-distance freight; Nigerian railways suffered long neglect and are being modernised (Lagos–Ibadan, Abuja–Kaduna standard gauge).", "<b>Air</b> — fastest, for passengers and perishables; expensive, limited capacity.", "<b>Water</b> — cheapest for bulk international trade (Lagos, Port Harcourt, Onne, Warri ports); inland waterways (Niger, Benue) are under-used."]),
   ("Problems of transport in Nigeria", ["Poor and potholed roads; congestion in cities (Lagos); inadequate rolling stock on the railway; port congestion and delays; insecurity (kidnapping, banditry on highways); high cost of vehicles, fuel and maintenance; insufficient air and water transport capacity."]),
   ("Communication systems & importance", ["Postal service, telephone (mobile: GSM market liberalisation since 2001 brought cheap calls and mobile money), radio and television, the internet, and now 4G/5G and fibre broadband. Communication stimulates trade, banking (e-banking, POS), education (e-learning), governance and national integration. Problems: poor rural network coverage, high data costs, cybercrime, and inadequate broadband penetration."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define transport and communication.", "Compare road and rail transport advantages and disadvantages.", "State three problems of road transport in Nigeria.", "How has GSM improved the Nigerian economy?"])]),
 ("Money & Financial Institutions",
  [("Introduction", ["Money is anything generally accepted as a medium of exchange and a measure of value. It evolved from barter (which failed because of the double coincidence of wants) through commodity money to modern currency."]),
   ("Functions and qualities of money", ["Functions: medium of exchange; measure of value (unit of account); store of value; standard of deferred payment; means of transferring value. Qualities: general acceptability; divisibility; durability; portability; homogeneity; relative scarcity; stability of value; recognisability."]),
   ("Types of money", ["Commodity money (cowries in old Nigeria, salt, gold); coins; paper/notes; bank money (cheques, bank deposits); electronic money (cards, mobile money, transfers); near money (treasury bills, bonds)."]),
   ("Financial institutions in Nigeria", ["<b>Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN)</b> — the apex bank: issues currency, banker to government and banks, lender of last resort, controls credit and monetary policy.", "<b>Commercial banks</b> — accept deposits, grant loans, create money through credit creation (First Bank, GTBank, Access, UBA, Zenith).", "<b>Merchant banks</b> — wholesale banking for businesses; <b>development banks</b> — long-term finance (BOI, BOA); <b>microfinance banks</b> — small loans to the poor; <b>insurance companies, pension funds, mortgage banks</b>, and the <b>capital market</b> (Nigerian Exchange Group) for long-term funds."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define money and state its functions.", "Why did barter fail as a system of exchange?", "State four qualities of good money.", "Distinguish between the functions of the CBN and commercial banks."])]),
 ("National Income & Economic Growth",
  [("Introduction", ["National income is the total monetary value of all goods and services produced in a country in a year. Its measurement shows the size, performance and structure of an economy."]),
   ("Concepts and measurement", ["<b>GDP</b> (Gross Domestic Product) — total value of output within the country's borders. <b>GNP</b> (Gross National Product) — GDP plus net factor income from abroad. <b>NNP</b> = GNP minus depreciation. Per capita income = national income ÷ population (a measure of average welfare). Measurement methods: the output (product) approach, the income approach and the expenditure approach — all should give the same total if properly computed."]),
   ("Problems of measuring national income in Nigeria", ["Subsistence production is not counted; the large informal sector escapes records; unreliable and incomplete statistics; double counting; price changes distort real values (use of real GDP at constant prices); non-monetary and illegal activities are omitted; population figures are uncertain."]),
   ("Concepts in analysis", ["<b>Real vs nominal income</b> — nominal at current prices, real at constant prices; <b>standard of living</b> — the welfare level of the people; <b>economic growth</b> — a sustained increase in real national output; <b>economic development</b> — growth plus structural and institutional improvements (less poverty, better education and health). Development can exist without growth? Yes — if output rises but inequality deepens, welfare may not improve."]),
   ("Class evaluation", ["Define national income.", "Differentiate between GDP and GNP.", "How is per capita income calculated?", "State four problems of measuring national income in Nigeria.", "Distinguish between economic growth and economic development."])]),
],
}
